; music-cascade — a 7-pattern cascade for the toy LLM-fold (E-llm-fold).
; Plain NL patterns (IF / HOWEVER / THEN / BECAUSE), deliberately OUTSIDE the futon stack.
; The circumstance |ψ⟩ = "compose a 3-voice fugue exposition in C major."
; An LLM TURN folds these into a construction (the fugue) — see E-llm-fold.md / compose_fugue.py.

@flexiarg music/fugue-subject
  + IF: you are beginning a fugue and have no thematic material yet.
  + HOWEVER: the piece needs one memorable idea that every voice can carry.
  + THEN: state a SUBJECT — a short, singable line (≈1–2 bars) in the TONIC, in a single voice, unaccompanied.
  + BECAUSE: the subject is the genetic material every later entry, episode, and stretto is derived from.

@flexiarg music/subject-answer
  + IF: the subject has been stated in the tonic and a second voice must enter.
  + HOWEVER: a literal repeat would not move; a free transposition could tip the ear into a new key.
  + THEN: bring the second voice in with the ANSWER — the subject transposed to the DOMINANT (a real answer
    transposes exactly up a 5th; a TONAL answer adjusts a leading scale-degree-5→1 leap so the home key holds).
  + BECAUSE: tonic-then-dominant statement is what makes the texture read as a fugue and frames the key.

@flexiarg music/countersubject
  + IF: the answer is entering in a second voice and the first voice has finished the subject.
  + HOWEVER: the first voice must keep saying something that fits the answer, not fall silent or clash.
  + THEN: give the first voice a COUNTERSUBJECT against the answer — ideally in invertible counterpoint
    (it must still work if the two lines later swap which is on top).
  + BECAUSE: invertibility lets the same pair of lines be reused, top-or-bottom, at every future entry.

@flexiarg music/exposition-entries
  + IF: some voices have not yet stated the subject.
  + HOWEVER: an exposition is only complete when EVERY voice has carried the subject once.
  + THEN: bring each remaining voice in with the subject, alternating TONIC / DOMINANT entries until all
    voices have entered.
  + BECAUSE: the exposition's job is to introduce all the voices through the one shared idea.

@flexiarg music/episode-by-sequence
  + IF: you are between subject entries and the texture would stall if you just stacked entries.
  + HOWEVER: you need motion and a way to relink keys without stating the whole subject again.
  + THEN: spin a short EPISODE by taking a fragment of the subject or countersubject and SEQUENCING it
    (repeat it a step or a fifth lower/higher) to travel.
  + BECAUSE: episodes develop the material and carry the music between the tonal pillars of the entries.

@flexiarg music/voice-leading
  + IF: more than one voice is sounding and you must choose the next chord.
  + HOWEVER: parallel fifths and octaves collapse the independence of the voices.
  + THEN: move each voice by the SMALLEST interval available, favour contrary motion, keep strong beats
    consonant, and resolve tendency tones (leading-tone up, chordal 7th down).
  + BECAUSE: independent, smooth voices are what counterpoint IS; parallels make it sound like one voice.

@flexiarg music/authentic-cadence
  + IF: a section (here, the exposition) is reaching its end.
  + HOWEVER: it needs an unambiguous sense of arrival and closure.
  + THEN: approach the final tonic with a strong DOMINANT→TONIC (V→I) cadence, the leading tone rising to
    the tonic and any dominant 7th falling to the third.
  + BECAUSE: the authentic cadence is the strongest signal of "we have arrived home."
